# The worked cargo project (plan 09) builds in place, because the point is that
# it builds the way a user's crate does rather than as a workspace member. Its
# artifacts and lockfile are not source.
examples/*/target/
examples/*/Cargo.lock
# Transpiler output. `examples/*.sdx` is the CI-checked source of truth; the
# `.xrs` beside it is a build artifact, regenerated by `redox build`. The
# `.xrs` files under `tests/golden/` are expectations, not artifacts, and are
# tracked.
examples/*.xrs
# Plan 06's source map is the same kind of artifact, and `examples/*.xrs`
# does not match it — the suffix is `.xrs.map`.
examples/*.xrs.map
# Plan 44's pair corpus is the same shape: `benchmarks/pairs/*.sdx` and the
# hand-written `.rs` beside it are the inputs, and anything transpiled from
# them is an artifact. Eight of these were left behind by `redox build` runs
# during the plan's development before the rule existed.
benchmarks/pairs/*.xrs
benchmarks/pairs/*.xrs.map
Cargo.lock
Thumbs.db
charts/
config.json
config.toml
config.yaml
dev-debug.log
devenv.local.nix
devenv.local.nix
log/*
logs
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Plan 41: tree-sitter compiles the grammar into a machine-local shared
# object on demand. The generated C **is** committed, because editors consume
# it; the compiled artefact is not.
tree-sitter-redox/*.so
tree-sitter-redox/*.dylib
tree-sitter-redox/*.wasm
